{{Japanese name|Kusaka}}

'''Genzui Kusaka''' (久坂 玄瑞, ''Kusaka Genzui''; 15 March 1911 – 20 May 1986) was a Sanese politician who served as the 34th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 1966 to 1968. A leader of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), he is one of the most controversial figures in modern Sanese history.

Kusaka swept to power in the [[Okaiken general election, 1966|1966 general election]] following a massive voter fraud scandal that delegitimized the previous [[Okaiken Democratic Party|Democratic]] administration. His tenure was marked by radical "Statist" policies, rapid nationalization of industry, and a confrontation with the [[Diet of Okaiken]].

In 1968, his attempt to suspend the parliament and transfer legislative power to a "People's Select Committee" triggered mass protests known as the [[Quiet Revolution]]. He was removed from power on August 12, 1968, leading to the dissolution of the OSP and the eventual establishment of the Federal Republic under [[Yoshimizu Ohno]].

==Early life and education==
Kusaka was born in 1911 in a rural farming village in [[Iwate Prefecture]]. His family was impoverished by the tenant farming system, a background that fueled his lifelong resentment of the "landed elites" and the ''[[Zaibatsu]]''. Despite his background, he was academically gifted and won a scholarship to the [[Imperial University of Okami]], where he studied law.

During the [[Great Adonian War]] (1947–1952), Kusaka served as a logistics officer in the Imperial Army. He became disillusioned with the military government of [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]], viewing the war as a tool for the aristocracy to exploit the common people. He was repatriated in 1953 and immediately entered politics, joining the newly reformed Socialist Party.

==Political career==
Kusaka became known as a "firebrand" in the Diet. Unlike the moderate wing of the OSP, which sought to work within the parliamentary system, Kusaka belonged to the "Statist" faction. He argued that Western-style liberal democracy was a "sham" designed to protect the bourgeoisie and that true equality could only be achieved through a strong, centralized state that enforced the will of the masses.

He became leader of the OSP in 1960. Throughout the early 1960s, he capitalized on the growing economic inequality and the public's exhaustion with the corruption scandals of the ruling Democratic and Progressive parties.

==Prime Minister (1966–1968)==
===The 1966 Election===
In 1966, the incumbent Democratic Party government of [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] was rocked by allegations of widespread voter registration fraud in rural prefectures. Kusaka ran a populist campaign on the slogan "Smash the Corrupt, Save the Nation." The OSP won a surprise plurality, and Kusaka formed a minority government.

===The "Statist" Reforms===
Kusaka's premiership was radical and swift. He bypassed the usual bureaucratic channels, issuing "Cabinet Directives" to nationalize the energy and rail sectors. He established "Worker Councils" to oversee factory production, which led to a sharp drop in productivity and a flight of foreign capital.

By late 1967, inflation was rising, and the opposition parties (Democratic and Progressive) blocked his budget in the [[House of Officers]].

===The Constitutional Crisis===
Frustrated by the parliamentary gridlock, Kusaka made his fateful move in July 1968. He drafted the "National Salvation Decree," which would dissolve both Houses of the Diet and replace them with a unicameral "People's Select Committee," appointed directly by his cabinet.

Kusaka argued this was necessary to "bypass the obstructionist elites." His opponents, led by [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] and the young [[Honda Tadashige]], denounced it as a coup d'état and a violation of the 1870 Saito Constitution.

==The Quiet Revolution and Removal==
On August 1, 1968, Kusaka presented the decree to [[Emperor Wakayama]] for the imperial seal. In a break with tradition, the Emperor refused to sign, citing the lack of Diet approval.

News of the decree leaked, triggering the [[Quiet Revolution]]. Millions of Sanese citizens flooded the streets of Okami in silent, non-violent protest. The labor unions, which Kusaka had counted on for support, turned against him, launching a general strike to protect the constitution.

On August 12, 1968, facing a paralyzed nation and a loss of confidence from the police and security forces, Kusaka was formally removed from power. He was placed under house arrest, and the OSP was subsequently banned by the Diet for "attempting to subvert the democratic order".

==Later life and death==
Following his removal, Kusaka was tried for abuse of power but was pardoned in 1970 by the new Republican government as an act of national healing. He lived the rest of his life in seclusion in his home prefecture of Iwate, refusing to give interviews.

He died of heart failure on May 20, 1986. While vilified by the mainstream, he remains a cult figure among certain radical leftist fringe groups in Okaiken.

